  "ai_language_tip": "Speak clear, polite standard Hungarian (hu); German is sometimes understood by older staff in the west and Balaton area; English works in central Budapest, hotels and with younger staff, rarely at GP offices, kormányablak or village trades.",
  "english_ok": "sometimes — Good in central Budapest, Balaton resorts and hotels; limited among older staff, public clinics and municipal offices.",
  "politeness": "Formal 'Ön' (or 'maga') with strangers; open with 'Jó napot kívánok' and say whom you call for; on the phone close with 'Köszönöm, viszonthallásra'.",
  "name_tip": "Hungarian order is family name first ('Nagy János'); married women may use '-né' forms; ask for name order explicitly and spell foreign names letter by letter.",
  "phone_tip": "Domestic format 06 + area/mobile code: mobile 06 20/30/70 + 7 digits, Budapest 06 1 + 7 digits; international +36; read in groups.",
  "time_tip": "TRAP: 'fél nyolc' = 7:30, 'negyed nyolc' = 7:15, 'háromnegyed nyolc' = 7:45; dates are year-month-day (2026. 11. 25.); always read back in 24h.",
  "number_tip": "Nouns stay singular after numbers ('4 fő', 'két személy'); 'kettő/két' both mean 2; districts are written in Roman numerals (XI. kerület), not counts.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid lunch rush 12:00–14:00, dinner peak 18:30–20:30, calls after 20:00 and weekends for offices; GP practices (háziorvos) have fixed rendelési idő and phone hours.",
  "closed_days": "Shops generally open on Sundays (the 2015 Sunday ban was repealed in 2016) but close on public holidays such as 15 March, Easter Monday, 1 May, Whit Monday, 20 August, 23 October, 1 November, 25–26 December; 24 December closes early.",
  "booking_channels": "Online booking pages (e.g. Bookinda, Revino) and Google for salons and some restaurants; private clinic chains and dental systems online, public care via EESZT/háziorvos by phone; Wolt and Foodora for delivery; phone and Facebook Messenger still dominant for small venues and trades.",
  "voice_menu": "Operators (Telekom, Yettel, One), banks, MÁV, Magyar Posta and government hotlines (e.g. 1818) use keypad IVR; small venues answer directly.",
  "callback_habits": "Booking systems send email/SMS reminders; small businesses confirm by SMS, Messenger or a call back from a mobile.",
